QB.push(
{id:2131,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Your security team needs one instruction — \"never write credentials into source files\" — to apply in every repository on every developer machine, and it must not be possible for an individual developer to switch it off. Where does it belong?",
o:["A. `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` on each machine, distributed by a setup script","B. `./CLAUDE.md` committed at the root of every repository","C. `./CLAUDE.local.md`, added to each repository's `.gitignore`","D. A managed policy `CLAUDE.md` at the platform's managed-policy path, or the `claudeMd` key in `managed-settings.json`"],a:3,
e:"The **managed policy** scope is the broadest of Claude Code's four memory scopes and the only one an individual cannot remove: managed policy `CLAUDE.md` files **cannot be excluded** by `claudeMdExcludes` in any settings layer. It lives at an OS-specific managed path (for example `/etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md` on Linux and WSL) or inline via the `claudeMd` key, which is honoured **only** in managed and policy settings. All four scopes are concatenated broad to specific, so managed content loads before user and project text. Note the limit: `CLAUDE.md` is behavioural guidance, so hard blocking belongs in managed `permissions.deny`.",
w:"A — user scope is the developer's own file: editable, deletable, and excludable. B — a project file covers only that repository and can be edited or excluded downstream. C — local files are personal, uncommitted, and trivially removable, the weakest scope for policy."},

{id:2132,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A developer's `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` says \"use 4-space indentation\"; the repository's `./CLAUDE.md` says \"use 2-space indentation\". Over a week, Claude Code produces both. What is happening, and what is the fix?",
o:["A. Both instructions are concatenated into context, so Claude sees a contradiction and may pick either; delete the conflicting line","B. The project file overrides the user file at load time, so the inconsistency must come from a nested `CLAUDE.md`","C. Only one memory scope loads per session, selected by `--setting-sources`; pin it to `project`","D. Instruction conflicts are resolved by file size, so the shorter file wins"],a:0,
e:"Memory scopes **combine rather than override**. Every discovered file is concatenated broad to specific, so both lines are literally present in context, with the project file read after the user file. Ordering gives the more specific text recency, not authority: the docs state that if two rules contradict each other, **Claude may pick one arbitrarily**. Because `CLAUDE.md` is context and not enforced configuration, the durable fix is to remove the contradiction — audit user, project, nested, and `.claude/rules/` content periodically. Transferable principle: guidance you cannot make consistent is guidance you cannot rely on.",
w:"B — scopes concatenate; a project file does not delete or override user text, so no nested file is needed to explain the flapping. C — all applicable scopes load together, and `--setting-sources` gates settings and rules sources rather than reducing memory to a single winner. D — file size plays no part in resolution."},

{id:2133,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"You launch Claude Code in `repo/services/api/`. Which statement describes how memory files load at launch?",
o:["A. Only `repo/services/api/CLAUDE.md` loads; ancestor directories are ignored","B. Files load from the filesystem root down to the working directory, and within each directory `CLAUDE.local.md` is appended after `CLAUDE.md`","C. Files load from the working directory upward, with the first one found ending the search","D. Every `CLAUDE.md` in the whole repository tree loads at launch, subdirectories included"],a:1,
e:"Claude Code loads `CLAUDE.md` and `CLAUDE.local.md` from the working directory **and every directory above it**, concatenating them **root-down** so that instructions closest to where you launched are read **last**. Within a single directory, `CLAUDE.local.md` is appended after `CLAUDE.md`, making your personal notes the last thing read at that level. Files in **subdirectories below** the working directory are discovered but load **on demand**, when Claude reads files there. Run `/context` and check the **Memory files** list to see exactly what loaded.",
w:"A — ancestor directories are included; that is the point of the hierarchy. C — the search does not stop at the first hit, and the order is root-down rather than inward-out. D — subdirectory files are lazy-loaded when Claude touches files in them, not loaded at launch."},

{id:2134,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A monorepo has `packages/payments/CLAUDE.md` holding payment-specific conventions. A developer runs Claude Code from the repository root, runs `/context`, and does not see that file under **Memory files** — yet later in the same session Claude follows its conventions precisely. Why?",
o:["A. `/context` lists only user-scope memory files, so the project file was loaded all along","B. The file loaded but was suppressed from the listing because it exceeds 200 lines","C. Nested `CLAUDE.md` files below the working directory load on demand, once Claude reads a file in that subdirectory","D. Claude inferred the conventions from the code and never read the file"],a:2,
e:"`CLAUDE.md` files in directories **above** the working directory load at launch; files in **subdirectories below** it are discovered and injected **lazily**, when Claude first reads a file in that directory. That is why the file is absent from `/context` at startup and present after Claude touches `packages/payments/`. The same lazy behaviour applies to `.claude/rules/` files carrying `paths:` frontmatter. Consequence worth remembering: lazily loaded instructions live in message history, so **compaction summarises them away** and they reload only when a matching file is read again.",
w:"A — `/context` lists every memory file that actually loaded, across user and project scopes. B — an oversized file reduces adherence, and a file over 4 MiB is skipped entirely, but there is no silent-listing behaviour at 200 lines. D — inference from code is unreliable and would not explain conventions being followed precisely."},

{id:2135,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A 900-line project `CLAUDE.md` contains a TypeScript section, a Python section, and a Terraform section. Most sessions touch only one stack, but every session pays for all three and adherence has dropped. What is the best restructure?",
o:["A. Move each section into `.claude/rules/*.md` with `paths:` frontmatter matching that stack's files","B. Move each section into a separate file and pull them in from `CLAUDE.md` with `@path` imports","C. Move each section into `.claude/settings.json` under a per-language key","D. Move each section into `CLAUDE.local.md` so only interested developers load it"],a:0,
e:"`.claude/rules/` splits instructions into topic files, and a rule with **`paths:`** YAML frontmatter (glob patterns such as `src/**/*.{ts,tsx}`) loads **only when Claude reads a matching file**. That removes two stacks' worth of tokens from the average session while keeping the guidance available where it applies. Rules **without** `paths:` load at launch with the same priority as `.claude/CLAUDE.md`. Target under 200 lines per `CLAUDE.md`: content that loads on every request both costs tokens and dilutes attention across instructions that do not apply.",
w:"B — imports are expanded and loaded at launch, so the token cost is unchanged; they help organisation only. C — `settings.json` holds permissions, hooks, and env, not prose instructions. D — local files are personal and uncommitted, so team conventions would stop being shared."},

{id:2136,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team moves its \"always run the migration linter before editing schema files\" rule into `.claude/rules/schema.md` with `paths: [\"db/**/*.sql\"]`. Developers report the rule is followed early in a session but ignored after a long session compacts. What explains it?",
o:["A. Rules are read once at session start and are never reloaded","B. `.claude/rules/` is excluded from compaction protection because it is not a `CLAUDE.md` file","C. The `paths:` glob stops matching after compaction because the working directory is reset","D. Path-scoped rules enter message history when a matching file is read, so compaction summarises them away; they reload only when a matching file is read again"],a:3,
e:"Unscoped rules and the project-root `CLAUDE.md` are **re-injected from disk** after compaction. Rules with **`paths:` frontmatter** and nested `CLAUDE.md` files behave differently: they load into **message history** when their trigger file is read, so compaction summarises them along with everything else, and Claude Code reloads them only as it re-reads matching files (it re-reads up to five recently modified ones). If a rule must survive every compaction, **drop the `paths:` frontmatter** or move it into the project-root `CLAUDE.md`.",
w:"A — unscoped rules and the project-root `CLAUDE.md` are re-read from disk after compaction, so \"never reloaded\" is wrong. B — unscoped rules in `.claude/rules/` are re-injected exactly like project `CLAUDE.md`; the `paths:` scoping is what changes the behaviour. C — the working directory is unchanged by compaction and the globs still evaluate normally."},

{id:2137,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"To cut startup context, a team splits its 800-line `CLAUDE.md` into six topic files and replaces the body with six `@docs/*.md` import lines. `/context` shows memory usage essentially unchanged. Why?",
o:["A. `@` imports are resolved but the content is deduplicated against the codebase, so the saving appears only after the first file read","B. `@` imports are expanded and loaded into context at launch, so splitting changes organisation, not token cost","C. Six files exceed the four-hop import depth limit, so all of them were inlined as a fallback","D. `/context` reports the pre-import file size, so the real saving is not visible there"],a:1,
e:"`@path/to/import` is an **inclusion**, not a lazy reference: imported files are expanded and loaded into context **at launch** alongside the `CLAUDE.md` that references them (relative paths resolve against the importing file, and recursion is allowed to a **depth of four hops**). Splitting for readability is fine, but the mechanism that actually reduces context is **path-scoped rules** in `.claude/rules/`, which load only when Claude reads matching files — or moving reference material into a **skill**, whose body loads only when used.",
w:"A — there is no deduplication against codebase contents; the text is injected verbatim. C — the four-hop limit governs import depth, not the number of imports, and there is no inlining fallback. D — `/context` reports what actually loaded, which is precisely why the number did not move."},

{id:2138,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A repository's `CLAUDE.md` says \"never modify files under `infra/prod/`\". A developer running with `--dangerously-skip-permissions` has Claude edit a production Terraform file anyway. Which mechanism would have stopped it in that session?",
o:["A. A `PostToolUse` hook that reverts writes under `infra/prod/`","B. Rewriting the instruction in bold at the top of `CLAUDE.md` and repeating it in `CLAUDE.local.md`","C. A `PreToolUse` hook that returns `permissionDecision: \"deny\"` for edits matching `infra/prod/**`","D. A `permissions.ask` rule for `Edit(infra/prod/**)` in project `settings.json`"],a:2,
e:"**`CLAUDE.md` asks; hooks enforce.** Memory files are context, not configuration — the docs are explicit that to block an action regardless of what Claude decides, you use a **`PreToolUse` hook**. Crucially, `PreToolUse` hooks fire **before any permission-mode check, in every permission mode**, so a `deny` decision blocks the tool even under `bypassPermissions` or `--dangerously-skip-permissions`. Hooks can tighten restrictions but not loosen them: an `allow` decision still cannot override a settings `deny` rule. Design principle: the model proposes, deterministic code authorises.",
w:"A — `PostToolUse` runs after the write has landed, so it is cleanup rather than prevention. B — emphasis and duplication change probability, not capability; no phrasing makes advisory context enforceable. D — `ask` prompts are exactly what that flag suppresses, so the rule would never fire in that session."},

{id:2139,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which statement about Claude Code hook handlers is correct?",
o:["A. Hooks may only be shell commands, so network calls must be made from inside the script","B. Hooks are declared in `CLAUDE.md` frontmatter and inherit the session's tool permissions","C. All hooks run sequentially in declaration order, so an earlier `deny` prevents later hooks from executing","D. A hook can be `type: \"http\"`, which POSTs the same event JSON to an endpoint and reads the result from the HTTP response body"],a:3,
e:"Hooks are configured in a **settings file** (`.claude/settings.json` and the other settings scopes) under a `hooks` block, and a handler can be a shell command **or** an HTTP endpoint via `type: \"http\"` — the endpoint receives the same JSON payload a command hook gets on stdin and returns results in the same JSON format through the response body. Newer handler kinds include prompt-based and subagent-based hooks. Hooks from all sources **merge**: every registered hook fires for its matching event, regardless of which settings scope declared it.",
w:"A — an HTTP handler type exists precisely so you need not wrap the call in a script. B — hooks live in settings files, not `CLAUDE.md`, and run as external processes rather than Claude tool calls. C — matching hooks run in parallel and every command runs to completion before results are merged, so one hook's `deny` does not suppress its siblings."},

{id:2140,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A key is set in `.claude/settings.json` (committed) and again, differently, in `.claude/settings.local.json`. Which value applies, and how does that differ from `CLAUDE.md`?",
o:["A. The `settings.local.json` value wins; settings resolve by precedence, whereas memory files are concatenated additively","B. The committed project value wins, because shared configuration outranks personal configuration","C. Both values apply and Claude reconciles them at runtime, exactly as with `CLAUDE.md`","D. Neither applies; a key present in two settings files is treated as ambiguous and dropped"],a:0,
e:"Settings and memory layer differently. Settings resolve by **precedence** — managed settings, then `--settings`, then **project local** (`.claude/settings.local.json`), then shared project (`.claude/settings.json`), then user (`~/.claude/settings.json`) — with the higher scope winning for the same key. `CLAUDE.md` files are **additive**: every scope's text lands in context together. Nuance worth knowing: some keys are unioned rather than replaced — `permissions.allow` and `claudeMdExcludes` **merge across layers** — and a few security-sensitive keys honour the most restrictive value from any scope.",
w:"B — local scope sits above shared project scope precisely so a developer can override a committed default on their own machine. C — runtime reconciliation by the model is how conflicting `CLAUDE.md` text behaves; settings are resolved deterministically by the client. D — there is no ambiguity rule; duplicate keys are normal and resolved by scope."},

{id:2141,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A non-fork subagent is spawned to investigate a failing test. What does its initial context contain?",
o:["A. The parent conversation so far, plus the subagent's own system prompt","B. Its own system prompt and environment details, the delegation task message, the `CLAUDE.md` hierarchy, a git-status snapshot, and any preloaded `skills:`","C. Only the delegation prompt; subagents receive no system prompt and no memory files","D. The Claude Code system prompt plus the parent's full message history, minus tool results"],a:1,
e:"A non-fork subagent runs in its **own context window** with its **own system prompt** — the agent file's body plus environment details, **not** the Claude Code system prompt — and its **own tool permissions**. Its startup context holds the delegation task message, every level of the `CLAUDE.md` hierarchy the main conversation loads, a git-status snapshot, the full body of any skill named in `skills:`, and a roster of sibling agents. It does **not** inherit the parent conversation; only a **fork** does. The built-in Explore and Plan agents deliberately skip `CLAUDE.md` and git status to stay fast and cheap.",
w:"A — inheriting the parent conversation is the defining behaviour of a fork, not of an ordinary subagent. C — subagents do get a system prompt, their own, and they do load the `CLAUDE.md` hierarchy. D — they receive their own system prompt rather than the Claude Code one, and no parent history at all."},

{id:2142,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team routes all codebase exploration through subagents. Main-session context now stays comfortably below the compaction threshold, but monthly token spend has risen noticeably. What is the correct reading?",
o:["A. Subagent delegation is being misused; correctly configured subagents reduce total tokens as well as main-window usage","B. The rise must come from `CLAUDE.md` being re-injected into each subagent, which is avoidable by deleting it","C. Each subagent runs its own context window with its own input and output tokens, so delegation trades higher total tokens for a protected main context","D. Subagent output is billed at a premium rate, so the fix is to run subagents on a cheaper model tier"],a:2,
e:"A subagent is a **separate agentic loop with its own context window**, its own input and output tokens, and its own startup content (the `CLAUDE.md` hierarchy, preloaded skills). Only its **summary** returns to the main conversation. So the benefit is **context isolation** — bulky search results and file contents never enter your window and never get re-sent on every subsequent turn — and the cost is **more total tokens**, because the boundary is paid for twice. Choose delegation when the intermediate output is large and you will not reference it again.",
w:"A — the docs frame subagents as context isolation, not total-token reduction; extra tokens are the expected trade. B — deleting project memory to save subagent startup tokens sacrifices the conventions that make the subagent useful, and is not the main driver. D — there is no premium subagent rate; setting a cheaper `model` per agent addresses cost, not the mechanism."},

{id:2143,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Two hours into a debugging session, a developer wants Claude to draft unit tests that depend on everything established so far — without the drafting work flooding the main conversation. Which mechanism fits?",
o:["A. A custom subagent with `tools: Read, Write` and a task prompt summarising the session","B. `/compact focus on the drafting task`, then continue in the main session","C. A `SessionStart` hook that re-injects the conversation into a new session","D. A fork, which inherits the conversation, system prompt, tools, and model, keeps its own tool calls isolated, and returns only its final result"],a:3,
e:"A **fork** is the subagent variant that starts with a **copy of the parent conversation**, along with the parent's system prompt, tools, and model. Ordinary subagents start fresh and would need the whole two hours re-explained in the delegation prompt. In both cases the child's tool calls stay out of the parent context and only the final result returns, so the main window stays clean. Rule of thumb: **fresh subagent when the task is self-contained, fork when the task depends on accumulated conversation state.**",
w:"A — a fresh subagent has none of the session history, and hand-summarising two hours into a prompt loses exactly the detail the task needs. B — compaction shrinks the conversation, but the drafting still happens inline, so its output lands in the main window. C — `SessionStart` hooks add context at session boundaries; they cannot replay a live conversation into a side task."},

{id:2144,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A repository contains both `.claude/commands/deploy.md` and `.claude/skills/deploy/SKILL.md`. What happens when a developer types `/deploy`?",
o:["A. The skill runs; commands have been merged into skills and the skill takes precedence on a name collision","B. The command runs; `.claude/commands/` is the more specific location and wins","C. Claude Code reports an ambiguous command and asks which to run","D. Both are concatenated and run as a single prompt"],a:0,
e:"Custom commands have been **merged into skills**. A file at `.claude/commands/deploy.md` and a skill at `.claude/skills/deploy/SKILL.md` both create `/deploy` and behave the same way; existing `commands/` files keep working, but when the names collide the **skill wins**. Command files accept the same frontmatter as skills except `name` and `paths`, which are ignored there because a command is invoked by filename. **Skills are the recommended form**, chiefly because a skill is a directory and can therefore carry supporting files.",
w:"B — precedence runs the other way; the skill supersedes the legacy command file. C — the collision is resolved deterministically rather than surfaced as an error. D — only one definition is used; there is no concatenation of the two bodies."},

{id:2145,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A `/release-notes` workflow needs a Python helper script and a two-page formatting reference alongside its instructions. How should it be packaged?",
o:["A. As `.claude/commands/release-notes.md` with the script and reference pasted inline","B. As `.claude/skills/release-notes/SKILL.md` with the script and the reference file in the same skill directory","C. As a `PreToolUse` hook that shells out to the Python helper","D. As three `@` imports at the bottom of the project `CLAUDE.md`"],a:1,
e:"A skill is a **directory**: `SKILL.md` plus any supporting files (`reference.md`, `scripts/helper.py`) that you reference from the body so Claude knows when to load them. That is the documented reason **skills are recommended over `.claude/commands/` files**, which are a single markdown file. Progressive disclosure is the payoff: the skill's `description` costs a few tokens per request and the **body loads only when the skill is invoked**, so a two-page reference is nearly free until needed.",
w:"A — a command file cannot carry sibling files, and inlining a script plus a two-page reference bloats the single file it can have. C — hooks fire on lifecycle events; nothing here is event-triggered, and a hook cannot be invoked as `/release-notes`. D — imports load at launch in every session, paying for reference material almost no session needs."},

{id:2146,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Where do you put a skill so that it is available in **all** of your projects and is never committed to any repository?",
o:["A. `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`","B. `./CLAUDE.local.md`","C. `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`","D. `.claude/settings.local.json`"],a:2,
e:"Skills live at three scopes: **personal** `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (all your projects), **project** `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (that repository, shared through source control), and plugin-provided. Personal skills sit in your home directory, so nothing enters the repo. When the same skill name exists at several scopes, one definition wins by priority — managed, then user, then project — unlike `CLAUDE.md` files, which are additive rather than overriding.",
w:"A — the project path lives inside the repository, so it is shared and normally committed, and it covers only that project. B — `CLAUDE.local.md` is uncommitted prose memory for one project and cannot define an invocable `/name`. D — settings files hold permissions, hooks, and env, not skill definitions."},

{id:2147,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A 3,000-line internal API style guide is relevant to perhaps one session in five. A developer proposes appending it to the project `CLAUDE.md`. What is the better placement, and why?",
o:["A. A skill, because a skill's description costs a little context each request while its body loads only when the skill is used","B. Project `CLAUDE.md` is correct; anything the team authored should be always-on","C. `.claude/settings.json`, so it is versioned with the rest of the configuration","D. `CLAUDE.local.md`, so only developers who need it pay the cost"],a:0,
e:"`CLAUDE.md` loads its **full content at session start and is part of the context on every request**, so a 3,000-line reference is billed continuously and dilutes attention across instructions that do not apply — which is why the docs target **under 200 lines** per file. A **skill** implements progressive disclosure: model-invocable skills contribute only **name and description** at startup, and the body loads on invocation or when Claude judges it relevant. Guideline: always-on rules go in `CLAUDE.md`; reference material Claude needs sometimes goes in a skill.",
w:"B — always-on is exactly the wrong default for material that four sessions in five never use. C — settings files configure permissions, hooks, and env; they are not an instruction channel. D — local scope shifts who pays but keeps the content always-on, and it stops being shared with the team."},

{id:2148,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A `/deploy-staging` skill triggers a real deployment. Twice this month Claude invoked it on its own after a developer merely mentioned deploying. What is the appropriate change?",
o:["A. Add `user-invocable: false` to the skill's frontmatter","B. Move the skill into `.claude/commands/` so Claude cannot see it","C. Add a `Stop` hook that cancels deployments Claude started","D. Add `disable-model-invocation: true` to the skill's frontmatter"],a:3,
e:"**`disable-model-invocation: true`** makes a skill **user-only**: its description is kept out of context entirely, so Claude cannot match it to your prompt, and the body loads only when you type `/deploy-staging`. The docs recommend it specifically for **skills with side effects** such as deploying or sending messages, with the bonus that such a skill costs zero context until invoked. For a hard guarantee rather than a reduced likelihood, pair it with permission rules or a `PreToolUse` hook on the underlying command.",
w:"A — `user-invocable: false` is the mirror image: it stops **you** invoking the skill and leaves Claude able to. B — command files and skills are the same mechanism now, so relocating it does not hide it from the model. C — `Stop` fires when Claude finishes responding, long after the deployment tool ran."},

{id:2149,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What does Claude Code's automatic context management do as a session approaches the context limit?",
o:["A. Truncates the oldest user messages until the window fits, preserving all tool output","B. Clears older tool outputs first, then summarises the conversation if that is not enough","C. Ends the session and asks you to start a new one with `/clear`","D. Offloads the entire conversation to a subagent and continues from its summary"],a:1,
e:"Claude Code **auto-compacts** as you approach the limit rather than ending the session, and the pass is two-stage: it **clears older tool outputs first, then summarises the conversation** if more room is needed. Your requests and key code snippets are preserved, while detailed instructions from early in the conversation may be lost — which is why persistent rules belong in `CLAUDE.md`, re-injected from disk afterwards. You can intervene with `/compact [focus]`, a **Compact Instructions** section in `CLAUDE.md`, `/clear` between unrelated tasks, or delegating large reads.",
w:"A — tool output is the first thing dropped, and it is the requests and key snippets that are protected. C — automatic compaction exists precisely so that a full window does not end the session. D — subagents isolate new work in a separate window; they are not the compaction mechanism."},

{id:2150,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"On long sessions a team repeatedly re-explains the same three architectural constraints after each compaction. Their current habit is to run `/compact focus on the architecture constraints` every time. What is the durable fix?",
o:["A. Raise the auto-compaction threshold with `/autocompact` so compaction happens less often","B. Put the constraints in a `.claude/rules/` file with `paths:` frontmatter matching the architecture directory","C. Write the constraints into the project-root `CLAUDE.md`, which Claude Code re-reads from disk and re-injects after compaction","D. Repeat the constraints in a chat message immediately after every compaction"],a:2,
e:"Instructions given **only in conversation** are message history, so compaction summarises them away. Content loaded from disk behaves differently: the **project-root `CLAUDE.md` and unscoped rules are re-injected from disk** after every compaction, as are auto memory and the plan written in plan mode. `/compact [focus]` steers a single summary; it is not persistence. Transferable rule: **anything that must hold across compaction and across sessions belongs in a file, not in chat.**",
w:"A — a later threshold delays the problem and buys the delay with a larger, costlier window; the constraints still vanish when compaction eventually runs. B — a rule with `paths:` loads into message history when a matching file is read, so it is summarised away too; dropping the frontmatter is what makes it persist. D — that is the manual workaround they are already doing, one step removed."},

{id:2151,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What happens to skills when a long session compacts?",
o:["A. Nothing changes; skill descriptions and bodies both live outside message history","B. Skill bodies are dropped permanently and must be re-invoked by hand","C. Only skills marked `disable-model-invocation: true` are preserved","D. Bodies of invoked skills are re-injected, capped per skill and in total with the oldest dropped first, while the startup skill-description listing is not re-injected"],a:3,
e:"After compaction Claude Code **re-injects the body of each skill you invoked**, truncated to a per-skill cap (about 5,000 tokens) inside a combined budget (about 25,000), dropping the oldest invoked skills first. The startup **skill-description listing is not re-injected** — only skills actually invoked come back. Truncation keeps the **start** of the file, so put load-bearing instructions near the top of `SKILL.md`. **Currency note:** the exact caps are implementation details that can change; the durable lesson is front-loading `SKILL.md`.",
w:"A — skill content enters the conversation as a message, so it sits inside message history and is subject to compaction. B — invoked skill bodies are re-injected automatically, within the caps. C — that flag controls who may invoke a skill and whether its description is listed; it confers no compaction protection."},

{id:2152,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"A reviewer has left the same comment — \"our repository uses named exports only\" — on four pull requests from four different developers, and each developer's Claude Code behaves differently on this point. Where should the convention go?",
o:["A. The project `./CLAUDE.md`, committed to source control","B. Each developer's `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`","C. Each developer's `./CLAUDE.local.md`","D. A chat message at the start of each session"],a:0,
e:"The scopes exist to answer \"who should see this?\". A **project** `CLAUDE.md` (`./CLAUDE.md` or `./.claude/CLAUDE.md`) is shared with the team through version control, so one commit fixes the convention for everyone, including future joiners. User and local scopes are per-developer and are exactly why one repository behaves differently for different people. The documented trigger is precise: **when a code review catches something Claude should have known about this codebase, that is a `CLAUDE.md` edit** — not a repeated correction in chat.",
w:"B — user scope applies to all of that person's projects and none of their colleagues', so it neither shares nor targets correctly. C — local files are gitignored personal notes; four developers would need four copies and the fifth joiner none. D — conversation instructions survive neither a new session nor a compaction."},

{id:2153,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A CI automation must guarantee that a formatting directive is present at the **system prompt** level for every non-interactive Claude Code run. A developer has put it in the project `CLAUDE.md`. Why is that not the same thing, and what is?",
o:["A. It is the same thing; `CLAUDE.md` content is appended to the system prompt at session start","B. `CLAUDE.md` is delivered as a user message after the system prompt; `--append-system-prompt`, passed on every invocation, places text in the system prompt","C. `CLAUDE.md` is read only in interactive sessions, so the fix is to set the directive in `env` in `settings.json`","D. `CLAUDE.md` loads too late to matter, so the fix is a `SessionStart` hook that prints the directive"],a:1,
e:"`CLAUDE.md` content is delivered as a **user message after the system prompt**, not as part of it — which is why the docs say Claude reads it and tries to follow it with **no guarantee of strict compliance**. For system-prompt-level text, use **`--append-system-prompt`**; because it must be passed on every invocation, it suits scripts and automation rather than interactive use. Related distinction: an output style and `--append-system-prompt` text both land in the system prompt, so they are **unchanged by compaction** rather than re-injected.",
w:"A — the delivery channel is a user message, and that distinction is exactly what the question turns on. C — `CLAUDE.md` loads in headless runs too, and `env` sets environment variables rather than prompt text. D — `SessionStart` hook output is added as conversation context, so it lands in message history, not the system prompt."},

{id:2154,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"In a large monorepo, launching Claude Code in your team's directory pulls in two ancestor `CLAUDE.md` files and another team's `.claude/rules/` directory, none of it relevant to your work. What is the intended remedy?",
o:["A. Launch with `--add-dir` limited to your team's directory","B. Rename the ancestor files so discovery misses them","C. Add glob patterns for those paths to `claudeMdExcludes` in `.claude/settings.local.json`","D. Delete the ancestor files and ask the other teams to use skills instead"],a:2,
e:"**`claudeMdExcludes`** takes glob patterns matched against **absolute paths** and skips the matching memory files. It can be set at any settings layer and the arrays **merge across layers**; putting it in `settings.local.json` keeps the exclusion on your machine only. One hard limit: **managed policy `CLAUDE.md` files cannot be excluded**, so organisation-wide instructions always apply. Note that `--add-dir` does the opposite — it grants access to extra directories, and their memory files are not even loaded unless you set `CLAUDE_CODE_ADDITIONAL_DIRECTORIES_CLAUDE_MD`.",
w:"A — `--add-dir` adds directories to Claude's reach; it does not prune ancestor memory discovery. B — renaming other teams' files breaks their setup and is not a supported mechanism. D — deleting shared files is destructive, and skills would not restore the always-on guidance those teams rely on."},

{id:2155,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A developer insists Claude Code is ignoring a convention they wrote in `CLAUDE.md`. What is the correct first diagnostic step?",
o:["A. Run `/memory` and confirm the file appears in the list of memory locations","B. Re-run `/init` to regenerate `CLAUDE.md` from the codebase","C. Add the convention to `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` as well, so it loads from two scopes","D. Run `/context` and check the **Memory files** list to confirm the file actually loaded this session"],a:3,
e:"Debug **loading before wording**. `/context` reports what is actually in this session's window, including a **Memory files** list — if the file is not there, Claude cannot see it and no amount of rephrasing helps. Only once loading is confirmed do you move to the usual causes: vague instructions, contradictions across scopes, or a nested or path-scoped file whose trigger has not fired. The `InstructionsLoaded` hook can log exactly which instruction files loaded, when, and why.",
w:"A — `/memory` lists and opens memory **locations**, including entries for files that do not exist yet, so it cannot confirm what loaded. B — `/init` proposes improvements to an existing file, and regenerating content does not answer whether the file reaches context. C — duplicating text into a second scope adds tokens and risks contradiction without diagnosing anything."},

{id:2156,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A checked-in `CLAUDE.md` has grown past 600 lines and adherence is slipping. Which content should be cut first?",
o:["A. Directory layouts, dependency lists, and architecture overviews, keeping pitfalls, rationale, and conventions that differ from tool defaults","B. The build and test commands, since Claude can discover them by reading `package.json`","C. Anything written more than three months ago, on the assumption it is stale","D. The specific, verifiable rules, keeping the high-level principles that generalise"],a:0,
e:"`CLAUDE.md` loads in full at session start and is **part of the context on every request**, so every line is billed continuously and competes for attention — hence the target of **under 200 lines**. The `/doctor` trim check encodes the right heuristic: cut what Claude can **derive from the codebase** (directory layouts, dependency lists, architecture overviews) and keep what it cannot — **pitfalls, rationale, and conventions that differ from tool defaults**. Overflow that must stay moves into path-scoped rules or skills, which load conditionally.",
w:"B — build and test commands are exactly the always-needed facts `CLAUDE.md` exists for, and rediscovery costs file reads every session. C — age is not a proxy for value; a two-year-old convention may be the most load-bearing line in the file. D — vague principles are the least-followed content, while specific verifiable rules such as \"use 2-space indentation\" are what drive adherence."},

{id:2157,d:"D7",s:"Claude Code Operation",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team adds a `PostToolUse` hook that runs the full test suite after every edit and returns the entire output to Claude. Sessions now compact every twenty minutes. What is the mechanism?",
o:["A. Hooks execute inside the context window, so every hook invocation costs tokens whether or not it returns output","B. Hooks cost zero context by themselves, but output a hook returns is added to the conversation as text Claude reads","C. `PostToolUse` hooks are re-injected from disk after each compaction, compounding the cost","D. Test output is stored as tool results, which are exempt from compaction and accumulate"],a:1,
e:"A hook's context cost is **zero unless it returns output** — the command runs outside the conversation. But returned output **lands in context as messages Claude reads**, which is the point of a `PostToolUse` linter and the trap when the payload is a full test log. Fixes: return only failures or a short summary, or return nothing and let Claude run tests when it needs them. Related mechanic: context a hook added earlier is **summarised with the rest of the conversation** at compaction, not re-injected.",
w:"A — hooks run as external processes, so the invocation itself costs nothing. C — only `SessionStart` hooks matching the `compact` source are re-run after compaction; `PostToolUse` output is simply summarised with the conversation. D — tool results are the **first** thing automatic context management clears, the opposite of exempt."},

{id:2158,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"After a refactor of the client wrapper, answer quality drops sharply across all endpoints. A prompt engineer starts rewriting the prompt. What should be done first, and why?",
o:["A. Lower `output_config.effort` to `low` and compare outputs, isolating reasoning depth as the variable","B. Retry the failing requests three times each; if quality varies, the model is the cause","C. Capture and diff the exact serialised request body actually sent — `system`, `messages`, `tools` — against a known-good request","D. Switch to a larger model tier; if quality recovers, the prompt was under-specified"],a:2,
e:"Before you can debug **model quality** you must rule out an **integration fault**, and the only way is to inspect what your code actually put on the wire. Log or diff the serialised request — the top-level `system` parameter, the full `messages` array with roles and block types, and `tools` — plus the **raw unparsed response**. A refactor that drops `system`, reorders `messages`, or omits tool definitions produces exactly this symptom while the prompt text in your repository looks untouched. Principle: **verify the request before you blame the model.**",
w:"A — tuning an effort control assumes the request is correct, which is the untested premise, and it cannot explain a step change tied to a refactor. B — retries probe flakiness rather than a systematic regression; a deterministic wiring bug reproduces every time. D — a bigger model may paper over a missing system prompt, hiding the defect while raising cost."},

{id:2159,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A client handles responses with `if stop_reason == \"end_turn\" ... elif \"max_tokens\" ... elif \"stop_sequence\" ... elif \"tool_use\" ... else raise UnknownStopReason`. It has begun raising in production on a small fraction of calls. What is the design error?",
o:["A. `stop_reason` should never be branched on; the presence of a `tool_use` content block is the only reliable signal","B. The comparison must be made against `stop_details.category` rather than `stop_reason`","C. `stop_reason` arrives in `message_stop`, so a non-streaming client reads a stale value","D. `stop_reason` is versioned as an extensible enum, so a client needs a safe default branch instead of treating unknown values as fatal"],a:3,
e:"`stop_reason` currently carries seven values — `end_turn`, `max_tokens`, `stop_sequence`, `tool_use`, `pause_turn`, `refusal`, `model_context_window_exceeded` — and the **versioning policy explicitly permits the enum to grow**. A client that raises on anything unrecognised converts a backward-compatible addition into an outage. Handle the values you act on and route the rest through a **default branch** that logs and degrades gracefully. **Currency note:** `refusal` and `model_context_window_exceeded` are recent additions that broke exactly this pattern in older clients.",
w:"A — `stop_reason` is the documented first diagnostic; inferring intent from block presence loses the truncation and refusal cases entirely. B — `stop_details` is populated **only** when `stop_reason` is `refusal` and is `null` otherwise, so it cannot serve as the primary branch. C — in streaming the final `stop_reason` arrives in `message_delta`, not `message_stop`, and a non-streaming response carries it directly on the message."},

{id:2160,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A tool-using integration returns short, unhelpful answers such as \"Let me look that up.\" Logs show `stop_reason: \"tool_use\"` on those responses and no follow-up request. What is happening?",
o:["A. The client treats the first response as final; on `tool_use` it must append the assistant turn, execute the tool, and send the result back as a `tool_result` block in a `user` message","B. The tool definition's `input_schema` is invalid, so the model produced a placeholder and stopped","C. `max_tokens` is too low, truncating the response before the tool call completed","D. `tool_choice` is set to `auto`, which lets the model decline to finish its answer"],a:0,
e:"`stop_reason: \"tool_use\"` means the model **paused to ask for a tool**, not that the turn is over. The loop continues: append the assistant message containing the `tool_use` block, run the tool, then send a **new `user` message containing a `tool_result` block with a matching `tool_use_id`**. There is no `tool` role. A client that renders the paused turn straight to the user shows a preamble and drops the real answer. Diagnostic habit: **`stop_reason` tells you whose move it is**, and `tool_use` is always your move.",
w:"B — a malformed `input_schema` is rejected at request time with a 400, not answered with a partial turn. C — truncation surfaces as `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`, and the log shows `tool_use`. D — `auto` lets the model choose whether to call a tool; it does not permit abandoning a turn, and the model did call one."},

{id:2161,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A response comes back with `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`. What does it indicate?",
o:["A. The request body exceeded the transport size limit and should be split","B. Generation ran into the model's context window, which bounds the request plus everything generated, thinking tokens included","C. `max_tokens` was set above the model's ceiling","D. The organisation's configured spend limit was reached mid-generation"],a:1,
e:"The context window bounds the **request plus everything generated, thinking tokens included** — output is not a separate budget. When generation collides with that ceiling, the response ends with **`stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`**. In agent loops this is the classic late-run failure: the API is **stateless**, so every iteration resends the whole accumulated history and tool results until the window is consumed. Remedies are architectural — prune or summarise history and keep bulky tool output out of the transcript.",
w:"A — an oversized body returns **413 `request_too_large`**, a transport-level error rather than a stop reason. C — that is a 400 `invalid_request_error` raised at validation time, before any generation, with no silent clamping. D — a configured org or workspace spend limit returns a **400** and a usage-tier monthly cap a **429**; neither is expressed as a stop reason."},

{id:2162,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"How should a client detect that Claude declined a request, and what extra field is available when it does?",
o:["A. Match the response text against refusal phrases such as \"I can't help with that\"","B. Check for HTTP 403 `permission_error`, which is returned for declined content","C. Check `stop_reason == \"refusal\"`; `stop_details` is populated with an open-set `category` in that case and is `null` for every other stop reason","D. Check whether `usage.output_tokens` falls below a threshold, since refusals are short"],a:2,
e:"Refusals are a **structured signal**, not a string to grep. `stop_reason: \"refusal\"` is the reliable check, and it is the **only** stop reason for which **`stop_details`** is populated — carrying an **open-set `category`**, so unknown categories must be handled gracefully. String matching breaks across languages, phrasings, and model versions. Operationally, a **rising rate of `refusal`** is a monitorable metric, which is possible only because the signal is a field rather than prose.",
w:"A — phrase matching is brittle across locales and model revisions, and misfires on legitimate content that quotes a refusal. B — 403 `permission_error` concerns API access rights, not content decisions; a refusal is a successful 200 response. D — short outputs have many causes, and a refusal can be long and explanatory."},

{id:2163,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A JSON extraction pipeline logs three distinct failure shapes: (i) valid JSON preceded by a conversational sentence, (ii) JSON that stops mid-object, (iii) JSON that parses cleanly but carries a wrong invoice total. A developer proposes one fix: a regex that pulls out the first `{...}` block. What is wrong with that plan?",
o:["A. Nothing; extracting the first JSON object handles all three shapes","B. Regex cannot match nested braces, so the plan fails on structural grounds alone","C. All three are truncation, so raising `max_tokens` resolves the whole class","D. The three shapes have three different root causes — formatting, truncation, and reasoning quality — so they need different fixes, and one of them is not a parsing problem at all"],a:3,
e:"**Classify parse failures by mode before fixing them.** A prose wrapper is an **output-format** problem, solved properly with `output_config.format` = `{\"type\":\"json_schema\", ...}` or `strict: true` on a tool, both of which use **constrained decoding**. JSON stopping mid-object is **truncation** — confirm with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`, then raise the cap or shrink the payload. Schema-valid-but-wrong values are a **model-quality** problem no parser can touch; that needs prompt work and an eval. A salvage regex hides mode (i), silently corrupts mode (ii), and is irrelevant to mode (iii).",
w:"A — it cannot repair a truncated object and cannot detect a wrong value inside well-formed JSON. B — brace nesting is a real limitation, but the decisive objection is that the three modes need three different remedies. C — only shape (ii) is truncation; a prose preamble and a wrong total are unrelated to the token cap."},

{id:2164,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which is the current mechanism for guaranteeing schema-valid JSON from the Messages API?",
o:["A. `output_config.format` set to `{\"type\":\"json_schema\", \"schema\": {...}}`, or `strict: true` on a tool definition","B. Prefill the assistant turn with `{` and stop on `}`","C. Define a tool whose `input_schema` is the target schema and force it with `tool_choice: {\"type\":\"any\"}`","D. Ask for JSON in the `system` prompt and validate with a retry loop"],a:0,
e:"Structured output is a **native feature**: `output_config.format` with `{\"type\":\"json_schema\",\"schema\":{...}}` guarantees schema-valid output through **constrained decoding**, with no beta header required; the tool-side equivalent is **`strict: true`** on a tool definition. **Currency note:** this **supersedes** the old force-a-schema-bearing-tool workaround, and **assistant prefill now returns a 400** on current models, so the `{`-prefill trick is legacy knowledge only.",
w:"B — prefill is rejected with a 400 on current models; recognise it as a dated pattern, never a recommendation. C — this is the superseded workaround, and forced `tool_choice` carries its own failure modes: it errors under manual extended thinking and 400s on some models. D — prompting plus retries lowers the failure rate but guarantees nothing, and multiplies cost and latency."},

{id:2165,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An eval suite compares generated support replies against stored golden strings. It goes red on almost every prompt tweak, engineers have learned to re-baseline it without reading the diff, and a real regression shipped last month. What is the fix?",
o:["A. Raise the pass threshold to a fuzzy string-similarity score above 0.9","B. Assert verifiable properties of each output — required fields present, the cited order ID matching the input, no promise of a refund, length within bounds — instead of exact text","C. Set the sampling controls to their most deterministic values so outputs stop varying","D. Reduce the suite to the ten cases that historically caught bugs"],a:1,
e:"Generation is not deterministic, so **exact-match assertions produce noise that trains teams to ignore the suite** — precisely the failure here. Assert **properties that are verifiable and stable under paraphrase**: schema conformance, presence of required facts, grounding in the input, absence of forbidden claims, bounds on length or latency. Add an LLM judge with a rubric only where a property is genuinely subjective. **Currency note:** determinism is not available as an escape hatch — `temperature`, `top_p`, and `top_k` are deprecated and rejected with a **400** on current models, and output was never fully deterministic even at 0.",
w:"A — similarity scoring still tracks wording rather than correctness; it accepts fluent wrong answers and rejects correct rewrites. C — those sampling controls now return 400, and even where still supported they never gave bit-identical output. D — shrinking to historically lucky cases is textbook overfitting and worsens coverage of the regression they missed."},

{id:2166,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team runs its eval suite in CI on every pull request. Quality still regressed overnight with no merges. Investigation shows the model ID and system prompt are read from a runtime configuration service that an on-call engineer changed. What must change?",
o:["A. Pin the model ID to a `-latest` alias so configuration changes cannot alter behaviour","B. Increase eval frequency to hourly against production traffic","C. Require two approvals on code pull requests","D. Move the model ID and system prompt into source so a person reviews them, and gate the eval suite on the configuration deploy as well as on code merges"],a:3,
e:"The reason evals belong in CI is that **prompt and model configuration change application behaviour without touching reviewed code**. If those values live behind a runtime flag, the CI gate has a hole exactly the size of the flag: put them under review, or make the configuration path run the same eval gate before it takes effect — ideally both. **Currency note:** there are **no `-latest` aliases**; current model IDs are dateless pinned snapshots, and pinning fixes weights but not serving infrastructure, so it is no substitute for a gate.",
w:"A — `-latest` aliases do not exist, and pinning an ID does nothing about a prompt someone edits in a configuration service. B — production monitoring detects a regression after users have met it, whereas the requirement here is a pre-deploy gate. C — more approvals on code do not touch the pathway that actually changed behaviour."},

{id:2167,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A prompt revision leaves the held-out eval score unmoved at 0.91, so it ships. Support tickets about refund questions then triple, while every other topic stays quiet. What did the evaluation miss?",
o:["A. The held-out set was too small for 0.91 to be meaningful","B. Held-out sets cannot detect prompt regressions, only model regressions","C. An aggregate score can hold steady while one category regresses and another improves; the comparison needed a per-category breakdown","D. The score should have been computed with an LLM judge rather than programmatic assertions"],a:2,
e:"A single aggregate number **averages away** exactly the signal you need: a drop on refunds offset by a gain elsewhere is invisible at 0.91. Build the held-out set from **real traffic**, stratify it by the categories your users actually bring — refunds, cancellations, shipping, account access — and compare **per category** before and after, watching for movement in either direction. Report the worst category, not just the mean. Principle: **evaluate the distribution you serve, sliced the way your users differ.**",
w:"A — sample size affects the confidence interval, but offsetting category movement stays invisible inside a single mean at any size. B — a held-out set sliced by category detects prompt regressions perfectly well; the slicing was the omission. D — a judge would have averaged the same categories into the same unmoved number."},

{id:2168,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Why should an evaluation set be built from real production traffic rather than from examples written by the team?",
o:["A. Real traffic reflects the input distribution the system actually serves — the messy phrasings, categories, and edge cases the team would not think to invent","B. Real traffic is cheaper to obtain than authored examples","C. Authored examples cannot be labelled reliably","D. Only real traffic can be used with an LLM judge"],a:0,
e:"Evaluation is only as good as its **input distribution**. Team-authored cases cluster around what the team already understands and systematically miss the truncated questions, mixed languages, pasted logs, and off-label uses that arrive in production. Sample real traffic, **hold it out** so no prompt iteration ever sees it, and stratify by category so slices can be compared. Keep authored cases too — as targeted regression tests for bugs already fixed — but do not let them stand in for the distribution.",
w:"B — collecting, scrubbing, and labelling production data is usually more work than writing examples, so cost is not the argument. C — authored examples are often the easiest to label; representativeness is the problem, not labelling. D — judges work on any input, and the source of the data is irrelevant to the scoring method."},

{id:2169,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A dashboard shows 99.9% HTTP 200s, stable p95 latency, no 429s, and a flat `stop_reason` distribution. Users report that the summaries have become vague and occasionally invent details. What does this reveal about the monitoring?",
o:["A. The users are describing a subjective preference change rather than a measurable regression","B. The dashboard measures transport and protocol health, but LLM quality degrades inside successful 200 responses; it needs sampled automated scoring of outputs plus alerting on the trend","C. Latency percentiles should be replaced with averages to expose the change","D. `stop_reason` monitoring is sufficient, so the reports must stem from a client-side rendering bug"],a:1,
e:"The distinctive LLM failure mode is **quality degrading while error rates stay healthy** — a vague or fabricated summary is a perfectly well-formed 200. Availability telemetry cannot see it. Close the gap by **scoring a sample of production outputs automatically** against the same verifiable properties your eval suite asserts (grounding in source, required facts present, forbidden claims absent), then **alert on the trend** rather than on any single score. Retain raw output for failures so a regression stays diagnosable after the fact.",
w:"A — invented details are a factual, checkable property, not a matter of taste. C — averages hide tail behaviour rather than revealing semantic drift; the metric family is wrong, not the statistic. D — a flat `stop_reason` distribution is entirely consistent with fluent, confident, wrong output, which is the problem."},

{id:2170,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Beyond final-output correctness, which set of signals evaluates an agent's **trajectory**?",
o:["A. Tokens per second, time to first byte, and connection reuse rate","B. Prompt length, tool-definition count, and system-prompt size","C. Tool calls versus a reasonable baseline, redundant or repeated calls, whether the run converged, and the termination reason","D. Cache hit ratio, batch discount realised, and requests per minute"],a:2,
e:"A correct answer reached by a wasteful or lucky path is a latent failure. **Trajectory evaluation** measures the process: **tool-call efficiency** against a reasonable baseline for the task, **redundancy** (the same call with the same arguments repeated), **convergence** (does the loop make progress or oscillate), and **how the run terminated** — a clean finish, a max-turns cutoff, or an error. These predict cost, latency, and reliability at scale, and they surface problems a green final-output assertion hides completely.",
w:"A — those are transport performance metrics and say nothing about the agent's decisions. B — request composition is a cost and context concern, not a measure of how the run unfolded. D — those are throughput and billing metrics, orthogonal to trajectory quality."},

{id:2171,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent run ends without an answer. The transcript shows the same `search_orders` call with byte-identical arguments six times, interleaved with reasoning, and the run stopped because it hit its turn limit. What is the most useful conclusion?",
o:["A. The tool is flaky; add retries with exponential backoff around `search_orders`","B. The turn limit is too low; raise it and the run will complete","C. The model tier is too small for the task, so escalate to a higher tier","D. The run failed to converge — identical repeated calls plus a max-turns termination point to a tool result the model cannot use, so fix the result content and the tool description"],a:3,
e:"Two trajectory signals together are diagnostic: **byte-identical repeated calls** mean the model is learning nothing from the result, and **termination by turn limit** rather than a clean finish means it never converged. The usual cause is a tool result that is empty, ambiguous, or shaped unlike what the description promised, so the model retries instead of adapting. Fix the **result content and the tool description** — the guidance is to improve descriptions and consolidate tools rather than add more. Log per-run tool-call counts, redundancy, and termination reason so this is visible without reading transcripts.",
w:"A — the calls returned; nothing indicates transport failure, and retries would only multiply an already-useless response. B — a higher limit buys more identical calls and more spend; the loop is not close to succeeding. C — a stronger model may mask a defective tool contract, at higher cost, while leaving the defect in place."},

{id:2172,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A RAG answer omits a policy exception. Retrieval logging shows the passage containing the exception was returned at rank 8, while the pipeline injects the top 5. What is the correct fix?",
o:["A. The retrieval stage surfaced the passage but the selection stage discarded it, so fix `k`, fix the ranking, or add a reranking step","B. Instruct the model in the `system` prompt to consider policy exceptions carefully","C. Raise `max_tokens` so the answer has room to mention the exception","D. Add the exception to the `system` prompt permanently"],a:0,
e:"**Debug RAG at retrieval first, and log the passages actually injected.** That log localises the fault: if the passage never appears, the problem is indexing, chunking, or the embedding query; if it appears but below the cut-off, the problem is **selection** — `k` too small or ranking that buries the relevant chunk — and the remedy is a reranker, a larger `k`, or better chunking. Only when the right passage is demonstrably in the prompt and still ignored does the prompt become the suspect. Prompting cannot recover text the model never received.",
w:"B — no instruction can make the model use a passage that was never in its context. C — the answer was not truncated; `stop_reason` would report `max_tokens` if it were. D — hardcoding one exception does not scale and leaves every other exception in the corpus equally unreachable."},

{id:2173,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A client sends a `tool_result` block whose `tool_use_id` matches no `tool_use` block in the conversation. What happens?",
o:["A. The block is silently ignored and the model answers without the tool result","B. The API returns a **400** `invalid_request_error`, so the tool result never reaches the model at all","C. The response carries `stop_reason: \"tool_use\"` again, prompting a retry","D. The API pairs it with the most recent `tool_use` block by position"],a:1,
e:"Tool results are returned in a **`user`** message as `tool_result` blocks whose **`tool_use_id` must match** a `tool_use` block in the preceding assistant turn — there is no `tool` role. An unmatched id is a request-validation failure: **400**. This matters when debugging \"the model never saw my tool output\", because it separates a **plumbing fault** (the call was rejected, or the result was assembled into the wrong role or block type) from a **behavioural** one (the result arrived and the model chose to ignore it). Check the raw request and the HTTP status before theorising about the model.",
w:"A — silent tolerance would make the bug undebuggable; the API rejects the request instead. C — a stop reason presupposes a successful generation, which never happens here. D — positional pairing is exactly what explicit ids exist to avoid, and parallel tool calls would make it ambiguous."},

{id:2174,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A single production call produced a bad response and you want Anthropic support to look at that exact call. Which identifier do you supply, and where does it come from?",
o:["A. `x-request-id`, a response header","B. The `custom_id` you set on the request","C. `request-id`, a response header, which also appears as a top-level `request_id` in error bodies","D. `message.id`, the identifier on the response object"],a:2,
e:"The request identifier header is **`request-id`** — **not** `x-request-id`, a common misremembering — and error response bodies carry the same value as a **top-level `request_id`** alongside `{\"type\":\"error\",\"error\":{...}}`. Log it on **every** response together with the model, `stop_reason`, and `usage`, and retain the raw unparsed output for failures. **Currency note:** the `x-` prefixed form is dated; assert `request-id`.",
w:"A — the `x-` prefixed name is the outdated form and is not the header returned. B — `custom_id` is yours to set on **Batch API** requests so you can match JSONL results, which are not order-guaranteed; it does not exist on a standard Messages call. D — `message.id` identifies the message object and is not the support-traceable request identifier."},

{id:2175,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Over forty iterations, a prompt's score on the team's 30-example development set has climbed from 0.71 to 0.95. Production quality metrics are flat across the same period. What is the most likely explanation?",
o:["A. The production metrics are lagging and will catch up once caching warms","B. Thirty examples is enough, so the production metrics must be instrumented incorrectly","C. The model was silently updated mid-experiment, cancelling out the prompt gains","D. Forty iterations against the same thirty examples has fitted the prompt to that set, so the score measures memorisation of those examples rather than generalisation"],a:3,
e:"Every iteration that looks at the same examples leaks information from them into the prompt. After forty rounds the set has become **training data**, and 0.95 measures fit to thirty items rather than capability on the traffic you serve. Guard against it structurally: a **held-out set sampled from real traffic** that iteration never sees, refreshed periodically, large enough and **stratified by category** to expose per-slice movement. Principle: **a number you optimised against has stopped being a measurement.**",
w:"A — prompt quality gains do not queue behind cache warming; the two are unrelated. B — flat production metrics agreeing with a suspicious dev-set climb is the signature of overfitting, not of broken instrumentation. C — model IDs are pinned snapshots with fixed weights, and a silent swap would not so precisely mirror a forty-round tuning curve."},

{id:2176,d:"D8",s:"Debugging & Error Handling",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"You are specifying observability for a Claude integration handling 200,000 calls a day. Which split between always-on and failure-only logging is appropriate?",
o:["A. Log `request-id`, model, `stop_reason`, and `usage` on every response, and additionally the raw unparsed output and the serialised request on failures","B. Log full request and response bodies on every call and sample nothing","C. Log only errors, with full request and response bodies, and nothing on success","D. Log latency and HTTP status on every call, and nothing else"],a:0,
e:"The always-on set is small, cheap, and high-yield: **`request-id`** for support traceability, the **model** actually used, **`stop_reason`** as the first diagnostic, and **`usage`** (including `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens`) for cost and truncation analysis. That is enough to spot a rising `refusal` rate, silent truncation via `max_tokens`, or a caching regression from aggregates alone. Reserve the expensive payloads — the **raw unparsed output and the serialised request** — for **failures**, where they turn an unreproducible incident into a diagnosable one.",
w:"B — full bodies on every call are costly at volume and multiply the data-retention surface, without adding signal you can aggregate. C — logging nothing on success removes the baseline that makes a rate change detectable, and many of these failures return HTTP 200. D — status and latency describe transport health only, and cannot distinguish truncation, refusal, or a caching regression."}
);
